      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e I think the fundamental question for this change is that whether commitGraph. changedPathsVersion requires us to keep commit graph data in memory consistent with the flag.\n\nI asked AI and it says: \"... the main reason to turn off commitGraph.readChangedPaths was protection against a subtle bug and corruption in the changed-paths Bloom filters, rather than saving memory.\"\n\n\"The primary reason this toggle exists—and why it was heavily utilized—dates back to a known bug in Version 1 Bloom filters. [...] Setting commitGraph.readChangedPaths \u003d false allowed users and administrators to instantly safeguard their workflows from reading these broken filters without needing to completely delete or rebuild the entire commit-graph\"\n\n\"While turning it off technically prevents Git from allocating the small chunk of memory [...], memory savings are almost never the motivating factor.\"\n\nAt least for now, we can live with the CPF still in memory but hidden.",
      "parentUuid": "24a2ed63_3cf4fd33",
      "revId": "9fdbbbc3b880e5bd6e026c035451b65c38f78693",
      "serverId": "d5d70762-12d0-45a1-890d-524b12d3f735"
